- Can we accelerate with divide-and-conquer optimization if quadrangle inequality holds?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests classic DP with an additional dimension (at most k intervals) and reconstruction—a common pattern in scheduling optimization problems.

## Key Concepts
- Weighted interval scheduling with a cardinality constraint (at most k intervals)
- Predecessor p(i) computed via binary search on sorted end times
- DP table with an extra dimension tracking the number of selected intervals
- Backtracking reconstruction to output the chosen jobs

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxValueKJobs(jobs, k) {
  const sorted = jobs.map((j, idx) => ({ ...j, idx })).sort((a, b) => a.end - b.end);
  const n = sorted.length;
  const ends = sorted.map(j => j.end);

  const p = (i) => {
    let lo = 0, hi = i - 1;
    while (lo <= hi) {
      const mid = Math.floor((lo + hi) / 2);
      if (ends[mid] <= sorted[i].start) lo = mid + 1;
      else hi = mid - 1;
    }
    return hi;
  };

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0));
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      const prev = p(i - 1) + 1;
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i - 1][t], dp[prev][t - 1] + sorted[i - 1].value);
    }
  }

  const selected = [];
  let t = k;
  for (let i = n; i > 0 && t > 0; i--) {
    if (dp[i][t] !== dp[i - 1][t]) {
      selected.push(sorted[i - 1]);
      t--;
    }
  }

  return { value: dp[n][k], jobs: selected.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests the ability to model selections of multiple disjoint subarrays with an exact count — a common dynamic programming pattern in interviews.

## Key Concepts
- **DP on prefix**: State dp[i][t] tracks the best total using the first i elements with exactly t subarrays
- **Prefix sums**: Enable O(1) calculation of any subarray sum
- **Backpointers**: Store decisions to reconstruct the chosen subarrays
- **Edge cases**: Handle when n < k or when all values are negative

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) pref[i] = pref[i - 1] + A[i - 1];

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const prev = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(null));
  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      if (dp[i - 1][t] >= dp[i][t]) {
        dp[i][t] = dp[i - 1][t];
        prev[i][t] = { skip: true };
      }
      for (let l = 1; l <= i; l++) {
        if (t > 0 && dp[l - 1][t - 1] !== -Infinity) {
          const val = dp[l - 1][t - 1] + (pref[i] - pref[l - 1]);
          if (val > dp[i][t]) {
            dp[i][t] = val;
            prev[i][t] = { skip: false, l, t: t - 1 };
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const result = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (i > 0 && t > 0) {
    const p = prev[i][t];
    if (p.skip) { i--; continue; }
    result.push([p.l, i]);
    i = p.l - 1; t = p.t;
  }
  return { value: dp[n][k], subarrays: result.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP over subsequences with a fixed length and pairwise costs, plus explicit reconstruction. It moves beyond contiguous partitions by requiring last-step linkage through prior picks.

## Key Concepts
- Subsequence DP with fixed length
- Min-over-predecessor recurrence
- Path reconstruction via parent pointers
- Time/space trade-offs and practical optimizations

## Code Example
```javascript
function solve(A, m){
  const n=A.length;
  const dp=Array.from({length:n},()=>Array(m+1).fill(Infinity));
  const par=Array.from({length:n},()=>Array(m+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) dp[i][1]=0;
  for(let t=2;t<=m;t++){
    for(let i=t-1;i<n;i++){
      for(let j=t-2;j<i;j++){
        const c=dp[j][t-1]+Math.abs(A[i]-A[j]);
        if(c<dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t]=c; par[i][t]=j; }
      }
    }
  }
  let best=Infinity,bestI=-1;
  for(let i=m-1;i<n;i++){ if(dp[i][m]<best){ best=dp[i][m]; bestI=i; } }
  const path=[];
  let cur=bestI, t=m;
  while(t>=1){
    path.push(cur);
    cur=par[cur][t];
    t--;
  }
  path.reverse();
  return {cost:best, indices:path};
}
```

## How It Works

Concise technical explanation of the mechanism.

## Key Points

- **DP State**: local[k], global[k] track ending vs non-ending subarrays
- **Transition**: local[k] = max(local[k]+a[i], global[k-1]+a[i]); global[k] = max(global[k], local[k])
- **Edge Handling**: cap K to n; initialize -inf; handles negative values

## Example

```javascript
function maxRevenue(A, K) {
  const n = A.length;
  if (n === 0 || K <= 0) return 0;
  K = Math.min(K, n);
  const global = new Array(K+1).fill(-Infinity);
  const local = new Array(K+1).fill(-Infinity);
  global[0] = 0;
  for (let x of A) {
    for (let k = K; k >= 1; k--) {
      local[k] = Math.max((local[k] !== -Infinity ? local[k] + x : -Infinity), global[k-1] + x);
      global[k] = Math.max(global[k], local[k]);
    }
  }
  return global[K];
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP formulation for selecting multiple non-overlapping subarrays, a common beginner challenge with practical reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- dp[i][t]: best sum using first i elements with exactly t subarrays
- end[i][t]: best subarray sum ending at i-1 for t subarrays
- Transitions combine extending a subarray or starting a new one
- Reconstruction via backtracking boundaries

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const end = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  for (let i = 0; i <= n; i++) dp[i][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      end[i][t] = Math.max(end[i-1][t] + A[i-1], dp[i-1][t-1] + A[i-1]);
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i-1][t], end[i][t]);
    }
  }
  return {best: dp[n][k]};
}
```

